@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:concept.How-to-differentiate-types-of-UI-blocks- a ds:Concept;
    ds:name "How to differentiate types of UI blocks ";
    ds:summary "This decision tree is helps us categorize UI blocks that share certain attributes: deciding whether we are talking about the same or separate UI blocks, if it’s a matter of inheritance, variants of the same UI block, or simply properties of a block.  ";
    ds:tier ds:global;
    ds:content "Given two things (X, Y) that share visual kinship: \n\nTEST 1 — AFFORDANCE  \n│  Does X enable a fundamentally different user action than Y?  \n│  (Different primary affordance: navigate vs act, input vs display,  \n│   dismiss vs inform, filter vs label)  \n│  \n│  Diagnostic signals:  \n│  • Would X and Y require different HTML elements for semantic correctness?  \n│  • Does one respond to user interaction where the other does not?  \n│  • Would a screen reader announce them with different roles?  \n│  • Could you describe X's purpose with a verb and Y's with a different verb?  \n│  \n├─ YES → SPLIT into separate components  \n│         (Chip / Label, Button / Link, Checkboxes / Radios*)  \n│  \n│         *Note: Radios/Checkboxes remain an open discussion.  \n│          The group has explored whether \"Choices\" as a unified  \n│          component better captures semantic function.  \n│  \n└─ NO → continue to Test 2\n\nTEST 2 — INHERITANCE  \n│  Does Y specialise X by narrowing its possibility space and increasing its specificity while  \n│  preserving X's affordance and anatomy?  \n│  \n│  Diagnostic signals:  \n│  • Y preconfigures properties that X leaves open  \n│  • Y restricts the API surface (fewer valid inputs)  \n│  • Y carries domain-specific opinions (branding, context)  \n│  • Y would NOT alter structural properties (spacing, layout) [BrandedLabel an edge case bc of historical reasons]  \n│  • Y's name naturally takes the form [Qualifier][Parent]  \n│    (BrandedLabel > Label, GithubHero > Hero, NumericalBadge > Badge)  \n│  \n├─ YES → Model as INHERITANCE (Y inherits from X)  \n│         Y receives all of X's characteristics.  \n│         Y is named with parent as suffix.  \n│         Y's documentation is separate and references parent's core docs.  \n│  \n│         Ontological note: This is a conceptual relationship.  \n│         Implementation uses composition (hooks, render composition),  \n│         not class inheritance.  \n│  \n└─ NO → continue to Test 3\n\nTEST 3 — VARIANT  \n│  Does the difference represent a design-time choice between  \n│  information delivery strategies under the same affordance?  \n│  \n│  Diagnostic signals:  \n│  • The choice is made AFTER the component/pattern has been selected  \n│  • The choice optimises information flow under constraints (not taste)  \n│  • The difference can be articulated as \"when you are in X situation,  \n│    use this form\" — i.e., the guidance is constraint-driven  \n│  • The semantic meaning and primary affordance are unchanged  \n│  \n│  Variant subcategories (provisional, not fully resolved):  \n│  \n│  a) COMPLEXITY VARIANT — Same affordance, different information density  \n│     (ProfileTag: short / medium / long)  \n│     (Timeline: simple / detailed)  \n│     (Pagination: basic / complex)  \n│     (Tooltip: dense / actions / data visualisation)  \n│  \n│  b) PLACEMENT VARIANT — Same affordance, different spatial arrangement  \n│     (Hero: image left / image right)  \n│  \n├─ YES → Model as VARIANT  \n│         Single component page with variant-specific guidance.  \n│         Guidance documents the constraints informing recommended choice.  \n│         Variants do not exist for aesthetic preference alone.  \n│  \n└─ NO → It's a property\n\nTEST 4 — MODIFIER FAMILY  \n│  Does the difference represent a cross-cutting communicative axis  \n│  that multiple components share?  \n│  \n│  Diagnostic signals:  \n│  • The variation is about status, emphasis, or hierarchy — not structure  \n│  • Other components in the system exhibit the same axis of variation  \n│  • Members of the family are mutually exclusive within one instance  \n│  • A component consuming the family must support ALL its members  \n│  • The variation is expressed through visual tokens (colour, weight),  \n│    not structural changes  \n│  \n│  Existing families: severity, emphasis, importance, criticality,  \n│  density, anticipation, lifecycle  \n│  \n├─ YES → Model as MODIFIER FAMILY membership  \n│         Component opts into existing family.  \n│         Modifier docs live at global level; component references them.  \n│  \n└─ NO → Variant  \n\n\n### Further references \n\n- Internal Canonical ontology [meeting notes](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1HHhTD80-nya5CkrWOzWitDsehDdILz0mkPPHzIySahs/edit?usp=sharing)";
    ds:knownEdgeCases "";
    ds:conceptType ds:concepttype.How-to-guide.
